# -*- mode:org;coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: look for on-demand macOS and windows cloud runners
#+AUTHOR: Pascal J. Bourguignon
#+DATE: 2026-07-23

The macOS (thalassa) and ms-windows (PC) runners are intermittent
laptops. The platform test lanes (test:clautolisp:{macos,windows},
verify:vl-registry:{macos,windows}) proved their worth on day one —
five genuine platform bugs — so reliable capacity is wanted.

* Task (pjb)

Survey cloud runner options usable on demand from gitlab.com CI:

- macOS: GitLab SaaS macOS runners (premium tiers), MacStadium/Orka,
  AWS EC2 Mac instances, Scaleway Apple silicon — pricing per job
  minute vs a reserved instance; arm64 (Apple silicon) preferred to
  match thalassa.
- windows: GitLab SaaS windows runners, a small always-on VM (Azure /
  Hetzner / OVH) with gitlab-runner as a service, shell = powershell.
- Requirements either way: sbcl + quicklisp installable at job time or
  baked into an image; tags to keep: [macos, arm64, shell] /
  [windows, amd64, shell] so the existing lanes pick them up
  unchanged; cost gate (manual trigger or scheduled) acceptable.

When one is adopted, consider promoting the platform lanes from
allow_failure to blocking on release tags.

* Survey — a CAD-free Windows BUILD host (pjb, 2026-08-21)

pjb: "pour builder une release de clautolisp […] on a besoin de le compiler
sur une machine windows, mais on n'a pas besoin de cad sur cette machine.
Alors on peut envisager une machine windows en docker ou qemu ou virtualbox."

That splits the Windows runner in two, and the split is the useful part: the
*CAD* lanes need the licensed GUI applications and therefore the physical PC;
the *build* lane needs only MSYS2 bash, SBCL, CCL and cmake — see
=scripts/release-windows.sh=, which is the whole of it. Only the second is on
the release path. Four ways to get one, measured rather than assumed:

** Windows containers on a Linux host — impossible

Windows containers need a Windows kernel; no Linux host runs them. What is
sold as "Windows in Docker" (=dockurr/windows= and kin) is a QEMU/KVM virtual
machine wrapped in a container — useful packaging, but it is the next option,
not this one.

** A KVM guest on poseidon — available TODAY, and the tooling is already there

Checked on poseidon on 2026-08-21:

: Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-9900K, 16 threads, vmx on all of them
: /dev/kvm            crw-rw-rw-      kvm_intel module loaded
: 125 GB RAM, 717 GB free on /
: qemu-system-x86_64, virsh, virt-install, docker — all installed

So the hypervisor question is settled: nothing needs installing. VirtualBox
would do the same job worse on a headless server; KVM is the right one here.

The cost is not the machine, it is the *licence*. Windows Server 2025
Evaluation runs 180 days, must be activated over the internet within 10 days
or it shuts down, and =slmgr /rearm= resets the 180 days up to six times —
call it three years of legitimate evaluation use, after which the VM is
rebuilt or licensed. A bought licence removes the question.

Second cost: provisioning. MSYS2 + SBCL + CCL + cmake + quicklisp, plus a
=gitlab-runner= service. A day's work, and *ours to maintain* thereafter.

** Azure free account — real, but the VM is too small

pjb's link, and the 750 h/month is genuine: it covers a single instance
running continuously. The catch is the size. The free tier's three VM sizes
are B1s, B2pts v2 and B2ats v2, all with *1 GiB of RAM*, and the offer lasts
*12 months*, after which it is billable.

One gibibyte to run MSYS2, compile libredwg with cmake and dump SBCL images
is almost certainly not enough — but that is reasoning, not a measurement,
and it is a cheap one to make: create the free VM, run the release build
once, and see. Worth twenty minutes before dismissing it, because if it fits
it is free for a year.

** GitHub-hosted windows-latest — free, unmetered, 4 vCPU / 16 GB, half built

The cheapest by a wide margin, and it needs no machine, no licence and no
provisioning:

- =ogamita/clautolisp= on GitHub is *public*, and GitHub's standard hosted
  runners are free and unlimited on public repositories.
- On a public repository =windows-latest= is *4 vCPU / 16 GB* (2/8 on private
  ones) — four times the Azure free VM's cores and sixteen times its memory,
  at no cost and with no expiry.
- =.github/workflows/release.yml= *already* carries the matrix entry
  =windows/x86-64 → windows-latest=. Its build step is a stub that echoes
  "TODO Windows packaging per documentation/windows-package-spec.md".

So the work is not "find a Windows machine", it is "fill in that stub", and
=scripts/release-windows.sh= is the script it would run.

The open question is *delivery*, not building. GitLab's =collect:release=
unions the per-target =dist/= directories, and a GitHub-built archive has to
reach it: either the GitLab job downloads the GitHub artefact (a token, and a
release that depends on a second forge), or the Windows products are attached
to the release from the GitHub side.

*That objection is weaker than it first looked* (checked against the Releases
API on 2026-08-21, after pjb asked whether assets can be added after the
fact). A GitLab release asset is *always a link* — GitLab never stores a file
for a release — and links can be added, repointed and removed at any time
after publication:

: POST   /projects/:id/releases/:tag/assets/links              (name + url)
: PUT    /projects/:id/releases/:tag/assets/links/:link_id     (name and/or url)
: DELETE /projects/:id/releases/:tag/assets/links/:link_id

(Names and URLs must be unique within a release, so a duplicate POST fails;
=make-gitlab-release.py --replace= already does DELETE-all-then-POST.)

So a release can be published with what exists and *gain* its Windows
products afterwards. Nothing has to be downloaded into a GitLab job: the
archive only needs a stable URL, wherever it lives.

And =direct_asset_path= is the piece that makes this tidy. It gives a link a
permanent GitLab-served address, =/releases/<tag>/downloads/<path>=, which
redirects to whatever the link currently points at. Our links are pinned to a
JOB ID today (=/-/jobs/<id>/artifacts/raw/…=), so re-running =collect:release=
strands them and the advertised URL changes; a =direct_asset_path= URL stays
put and is repointed underneath. Worth adopting whether or not the Windows
build ever moves to GitHub — see release-asset-links-use-artifacts-file.issue
for why the link form was already once the thing that broke releases.

** Recommendation

Fill in the GitHub =windows-latest= stub first: it costs nothing, the runner
is bigger than anything else on this list, and the scaffolding exists. Keep
KVM-on-poseidon as the fallback for the day the two-forge dependency becomes
uncomfortable — the hardware and the hypervisor are already there; only a
licence and a day of provisioning are missing.

Either way the physical Windows PC stays: it is the CAD host, and nothing
here replaces that.
